# an-undefended-base — plan 12 B7, the CONTROL.
#
# A core, a spawn, and nothing whatsoever in between.  Three waves walk
# in and stand on the core until the wallet runs out.  It is the number
# every other scenario in this phase is measured against: a base that
# falls in 161 ticks and a base that falls in 311 are the difference
# between "damage exists" and "defences work", and only the second is
# worth building.
#
# ── Why the control is not "the same map with the walls deleted" ──
#
# It nearly is, and deliberately: `a-defended-base.keys` is this file
# plus five `click`s.  `plans/12` § Invariant gate names the failure —
# *equal times = the scenario measures nothing, whatever it draws* — so
# the two runs have to differ in the defences and in NOTHING else.  Same
# ground, same core, same spawn, same wave list, same seed of a walk.
#
# ── The world ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
#   five rows of grass, sixteen columns:  (-3, -2) .. (12, 2)
#   core at (0, 0), spawn at (11, 0) facing west
#
# ⚠ **The width does not matter, and that is a measurement rather than
# an assumption.**  The same three waves against the same core give
# 161 ticks over five rows and 161 over thirteen — because the attack
# is a COLUMN however much room it has.  Everything east of the spawn
# and north or south of the band is scenery, so this file buys the
# cheapest world that answers the same.

echo an undefended base: nothing in the way, and the wallet is the clock

do toggle_mode

# The core is where the target is.
do cycle_kind
click 0 0
marker 0 0 target

# The spawn, eleven hexes east — clear of close_spawn_disable_radius
# (10) and well inside the scrambler bubble (25).
# ⚠ TWICE more: the place-kind cycle is spawn -> target -> tower ->
# spawn since plan 12 B5a gave towers a marker kind of their own.
do cycle_kind
do cycle_kind
do rotate
do rotate
do rotate
click 11 0
marker 11 0 spawn 3

count markers 2 2
do toggle_mode

palette 5
drag -3 -2 12 -2
drag -3 -1 12 -1
drag -3 0 12 0
drag -3 1 12 1
drag -3 2 12 2
count painted 80 80

# ── The waves: `numbers.json` § wave_system, first two ──────────
#
# 5 and 8 off the authored `wave_list`, spaced by the file's own
# `inter_wave_delay` of 15 s — which is 22.5 ticks, so 22.  Nothing
# here invents a number.
#
# ⚠ **The third wave was measured and dropped, and what it measured is
# the finding.**  Adding `wave 12` gives 161 ticks — the same clock, to
# the tick, in this scenario and in both defended ones.  Twelve more
# robots change NOTHING, because the drain is capped by the queue and
# not by the roster (see § The clock below).  So the scenario buys the
# two waves that fit in the gate's time budget and states why the third
# is scenery.
wave 5
count alive 5 5
wallet 200.0 200.0

tick 22
wave 8
count alive 13 13

# ── The clock ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# ⚠ **161 ticks is not thirteen robots' worth of nibbling, and the gap
# is this phase's central finding.**  Thirteen enemies arrive and only
# TWO ever drain the wallet: they come down one axis from one spawn,
# and on a hex axis the field offers exactly ONE closer neighbour, so a
# blocked enemy waits instead of stepping beside it.  Plan 11 F7 named
# that gap and declined to build the equal-distance sidestep; here it
# stops being latent and sets the whole clock.
#
# So the drain is a flat 2 pt/s — 1.333 points a tick — from the moment
# the first pair arrives, and every robot behind them is queueing.
# `tests/12_b7_the_clock.loft` prices it directly: a column of four and
# a column of twelve drain at exactly the same rate.
fall 800
ticks 156 166
wallet 0.0 0.0

# Nobody laid a finger on them.  This is the control's defining
# property and the reason `count alive` is here rather than in a
# comment: the moment something in this scenario kills an enemy, it is
# no longer the undefended base.
count alive 13 13

snap b7_an_undefended_base
